  simple: "For the last fifty to sixty years Ethiopia has been known as the Water Tower of East Africa, and it is the second richest African country in water-resource potential after the Democratic Republic of Congo. Yet its water resources degrade. Drought recurs every 3 to 5 years. Some Rift Valley lakes, such as Lake Abijata and Lake Cheleklektu, are disappearing or on the verge of disappearance. The volume and purity of Ethiopia's rivers are decreasing as sewage, organic wastes and fertilizers contaminate them, while the rapidly growing population demands more and more potable water. In response, the Federal government adopted a national strategy to conserve and manage water resources, covering watershed protection, wetlands, dams, pollution control and community participation.",
  detailed: "<p><b>The Water Tower under threat.</b> Ethiopia has been known as the <b>Water Tower of East Africa</b> for the last fifty to sixty years, and it is the second richest African country in terms of water-resource potential, following the Democratic Republic of Congo. But abundance on paper does not guarantee abundance on the ground: several worrying signs of degradation have appeared.</p><p><b>Recurring drought.</b> Drought is recurring every 3 to 5 years in Ethiopia. Such frequency means communities and herds barely recover before the next shortfall, straining both rural livelihoods and water supplies.</p><p><b>Disappearing lakes.</b> Some Rift Valley lakes, such as <b>Lake Abijata</b> and <b>Lake Cheleklektu</b>, are disappearing or are on the verge of disappearance. Their shrinkage is one of the clearest pieces of evidence that the country's water resource base is degrading.</p><p><b>Falling volume and purity.</b> The volume and purity of Ethiopia's rivers are decreasing. Pollutants such as sewage, organic wastes and fertilizers are increasingly contaminating the country's water resources. Contamination turns the water problem from one of quantity into one of quality as well, since polluted water is unsafe for households, livestock and irrigation alike.</p><p><b>Rising demand.</b> Due to the rapid growth of the human population, there is a crucial and growing demand for potable water. More people must share the same, and increasingly degraded, resource base, so shortages spread in both urban and rural areas.</p><p><b>The national strategy.</b> These and other factors have created the need for the conservation and management of water resources. In response, the Federal government adopted a national strategy to conserve and manage water resources in Ethiopia. The strategy works so that: major conservation, development and management projects include the costs and benefits of protecting watershed forests, wetlands and other key ecosystems; natural ecosystems, especially wetlands and upstream forests, are recognized as fundamental for regulating water quality and quantity and their rehabilitation is integrated into water projects; controlling environmental health hazards is a necessary condition in the design and use of dams and irrigation systems; and any proposed introduction of exotic species into water ecosystems is subject to detailed studies and environmental-impact assessment.</p><p><b>Protection and participation.</b> The strategy further promotes protection of the interface between water bodies and land, for example lake shores, river banks and wetlands; involves water-resource users, particularly women and animal herders, in the local planning, design and follow-up of water policies, programs and projects; recycles waste water when it is safe for health and the environment; promotes artificial recharge of ground and surface water; promotes effective farm-level water-management techniques for medium-to-large-scale irrigation schemes; and provides technical and credit support to the private sector in water resource development. Awareness has also been created through school syllabi and extension services.</p>",
  keyTerms: [
    { term: "Water Tower of East Africa", def: "The name Ethiopia has carried for the last fifty to sixty years, reflecting its water-resource potential." },
    { term: "Potable water", def: "Water safe for human consumption, whose demand is growing with Ethiopia's population." },
    { term: "Drought", def: "A prolonged shortfall of water supply that recurs every 3 to 5 years in Ethiopia." },
    { term: "Watershed", def: "The land area draining into a water body, whose forests and wetlands regulate water quality and quantity." },
    { term: "Wetland", def: "An upstream or interface ecosystem fundamental to regulating water quality and quantity." },
    { term: "Water pollution", def: "Contamination of water by pollutants such as sewage, organic wastes and fertilizers." },
    { term: "Environmental-impact assessment", def: "A detailed study required before exotic species may be introduced into water ecosystems." },
    { term: "Artificial recharge", def: "Measures to add water back into ground and surface water resources." }
  ],
